{"reference":"Judges 4","book":"Judges","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Within Judges","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Judges 4 belongs to a larger movement in the book: incomplete possession and covenant indictment give way to recurring deliverance stories, while later chapters move toward internal idolatry, atrocity, and civil war. This places the chapter's deliverance from Jabin within a book that traces an increasingly troubled national story.","verse_refs":["Judges 1","Judges 10:1-18","Judges 11:1-40","Judges 12:1-15","Judges 13:1-25","Judges 14:1-20","Judges 15:1-20","Judges 16:1-31","Judges 17:1-13","Judges 18:1-31","Judges 19:1-30","Judges 20:1-48","Judges 21:1-25","Judges 2:1-23","Judges 3:1-31","Judges 5:1-31","Judges 6:1-40","Judges 7:1-25","Judges 8:1-35","Judges 9:1-57"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_004_6aa46c8958855b54ace37477"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The account continues in Judges 5. Taken together, these chapters present Deborah as prophet and judge, Barak as the military commander, and Jael as the person who kills Sisera.","verse_refs":["Judges 5:1-31"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_004_2acb7463bc68562cb811f847"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}